  {
   "id": "math-0013",
   "bucket": "reasoning",
   "planned_path": "P5",
   "scion_answer": "To find the $2007^{\\text{th}}$ digit to the right of the decimal point in $\\frac{1}{7}$, we first determine the decimal expansion.\n\n**Step 1: Find the decimal expansion of $\\frac{1}{7}$**\n\nPerforming long division:\n- $1 \\div 7 = 0$ remainder $1$\n- $10 \\div 7 = 1$ remainder $3$\n- $30 \\div 7 = 4$ remainder $2$\n- $20 \\div 7 = 2$ remainder $6$\n- $60 \\div 7 = 8$ remainder $4$\n- $40 \\div 7 = 5$ remainder $5$\n- $50 \\div 7 = 7$ remainder $1$\n\nThe decimal expansion is:\n$$\\frac{1}{7} = 0.142857142857142857\\ldots$$\n\nThe repeating part is **142857**, which has **6 digits**.\n\n**Step 2: Find the 2007th digit**\n\nSince the repeating sequence has 6 digits, the $n^{\\text{th}}$ digit corresponds to position $n \\mod 6$ in the repeating sequence.\n\n$$2007 \\div 6 = 334 \\text{ remainder } 3$$\n\nSo the 2007th digit is the **3rd** digit in \"142857\", which is:\n\n$$\\boxed{2}$$",
